import{_ as n,C as r,o as t,c as i,a2 as o,b as l,w as a,a as h,E as d,a3 as u}from"./chunks/framework.yZB1U4Nl.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Why Vlo","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"why-vlo.md","filePath":"why-vlo.md"}'),c={name:"why-vlo.md"};function p(g,e,f,w,m,k){const s=r("Mermaid");return t(),i("div",null,[e[1]||(e[1]=o('<h1 id="why-vlo" tabindex="-1">Why Vlo <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-vlo" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why Vlo&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Vlo is durable execution that is tiny, performant, reliable, efficient and built to run at the edge. This page explains what each of those buys you, why one design principle makes all five possible, and what Vlo does not have yet.</p><h2 id="what-durable-execution-costs-today" tabindex="-1">What durable execution costs today <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-durable-execution-costs-today" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What durable execution costs today&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Durable execution keeps long-running work alive through crashes. It usually makes you pay five costs that have nothing to do with your product.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Cost</th><th>What it looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Heavy</strong></td><td>The engine is a server cluster, a large server binary or a hosted state store, and often a message broker as well.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Slower as runs grow</strong></td><td>Engines that resume by re-running the workflow from the top do more work on each resume as a run&#39;s history grows.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fragile resumes</strong></td><td>That re-run must behave identically every time. One clock read or random number in the wrong place breaks recovery, and you find out in production.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tied to one place</strong></td><td>A platform engine runs on one cloud. A server engine needs machines in a region you operate. Either way the engine decides where your work lives.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Costly while idle</strong></td><td>A cluster runs whether or not there is work, and waiting workflows often hold a worker or a slot.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="the-five-benefits" tabindex="-1">The five benefits <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-five-benefits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The five benefits&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="tiny" tabindex="-1">Tiny <a class="header-anchor" href="#tiny" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tiny&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Vlo is small enough to be a library in your app instead of a system beside it.</p><ul><li><strong>298 KB kernel.</strong> Rust compiled to wasm, 297,573 bytes. With the Node host it is under 600 KB. CI fails the build if the kernel passes 1 MiB or the runtime JavaScript passes 256 KB. See <a href="./limits#size-budgets-ci-gate">limits.md</a>.</li><li><strong>Nothing to stand up.</strong> There is no server, no cluster and no broker. You add a package and point it at a database.</li><li><strong>A closed model.</strong> Five event kinds, seven commands and seven statuses. That is the whole programming model, and projects cannot extend the kernel.</li><li><strong>A small surface for you, too.</strong> One config file, four CLI verbs, three adapters, and a hello-world in three files.</li></ul><h3 id="performant" tabindex="-1">Performant <a class="header-anchor" href="#performant" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Performant&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Vlo&#39;s speed comes from doing little work per step, and the amount of work does not grow with the run.</p><ul><li><strong>One transaction per step.</strong> The event, the new state and the next tasks commit together. There is no second system to coordinate with.</li><li><strong>Constant-cost resume.</strong> State is rewritten with every event, so waking a run is: load one row, verify its hash, continue. A run with 20,018 events resumed in the same time as a run with 38.</li><li><strong>No broker hop.</strong> A task is a row committed with the event. A worker in the same process skips HTTP entirely, and a polling worker is woken the moment a task is written.</li><li><strong>Small rows.</strong> Payloads over 256 KiB move to a content-addressed blob store, so the journal stays light.</li></ul><p>The measured numbers, their setup and their limits are in <a href="./performance">performance.md</a>.</p><h3 id="reliable" tabindex="-1">Reliable <a class="header-anchor" href="#reliable" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reliable&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A crash at any point loses nothing, and wrong state cannot pass unnoticed.</p><ul><li><strong>Commit before acknowledge.</strong> Nothing is confirmed to a caller or a worker until it is in the journal.</li><li><strong>A transactional outbox.</strong> Tasks, timers and child runs are rows written with the event. A crash between &quot;saved&quot; and &quot;sent&quot; is picked up on the next pass by any node.</li><li><strong>Leases and heartbeats.</strong> A worker that dies stops heartbeating, the lease lapses, and the retry policy decides what happens next.</li><li><strong>State that checks itself.</strong> Every checkpoint carries a hash that is verified on every load. A run rebuilt from its journal is compared step by step, and a mismatch fails the run at the exact event.</li><li><strong>Recovery never depends on re-running your code.</strong> Vlo resumes from stored state. Reducers must still be pure, but a mistake shows up as a failed rebuild, not as a silently different run.</li></ul><p>The full list of guarantees, and what each one does not promise, is in <a href="./reliability">reliability.md</a>.</p><h3 id="efficient" tabindex="-1">Efficient <a class="header-anchor" href="#efficient" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Efficient&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Vlo uses resources only while a step is actually running.</p><ul><li><strong>Waiting is free.</strong> Between events a run holds no compute, no open connection and no suspended stack. A run waiting two days for an approval is about 2 KB of rows.</li><li><strong>Workers scale to zero.</strong> Tasks are pushed over HTTP, so workers can be serverless functions that exist only while they work.</li><li><strong>No infrastructure tax.</strong> The journal is the Postgres you already run, and it is also the queue.</li><li><strong>One process to start.</strong> Orchestrator, API and workers are roles of a single process. You split them only when load calls for it.</li><li><strong>Efficient with model spend.</strong> The kernel meters tokens and cost on every event, including failed attempts. An agent that reaches its budget parks until someone tops it up. See <a href="./agents">agents.md</a>.</li><li><strong>Efficient with your time.</strong> One command for local development on a SQLite file, with no Docker and no cloud account, and generated deploy files for Cloud Run and Vercel.</li></ul><h3 id="built-for-the-edge" tabindex="-1">Built for the edge <a class="header-anchor" href="#built-for-the-edge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Built for the edge&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Vlo is designed so the engine goes to the data, instead of the data going to the engine&#39;s cluster.</p><ul><li><strong>A kernel with no I/O.</strong> It only decides. It returns a write-set of rows and the host writes them, so it runs anywhere wasm runs: a container, a serverless function, a laptop.</li><li><strong>A run lives where its journal lives.</strong> One journal and one writer per run gives total order with no consensus protocol.</li><li><strong>One durability model.</strong> Commit before acknowledge, everywhere. The edge is a place to deploy, never a weaker tier.</li></ul><p><strong>Status: the edge host is not built yet.</strong> Today Vlo runs on Node: Cloud Run, Vercel, a container or a laptop. A Cloudflare host with a Durable Object journal is the next platform milestone. Nothing in the kernel needs to change to get there, but until it ships, do not plan on running Vlo in an edge runtime. See <a href="./status">status.md</a>.</p><h2 id="one-design-principle" tabindex="-1">One design principle <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-design-principle" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One design principle&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A Vlo workflow is one pure function:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>(state, event) → (state, commands)</span></span></code></pre></div><ol><li>Something happens to a run: it started, a task finished, a signal arrived, a timer fired, or a child run ended. That is an event.</li><li>The kernel appends the event to the run&#39;s journal and hands it to your reducer with the current state.</li><li>The reducer returns the new state and a list of commands: run a task, set a timer, start a child run, wait for a signal, emit, complete, or continue as a new run.</li><li>The event, the new state and the commands are saved in one database transaction.</li><li>The host carries the commands out, pushing tasks to workers and firing timers.</li></ol><p>The five benefits are one chain of consequences from that function:</p>',29)),(t(),l(u,null,{default:a(()=>[d(s,{id:"mermaid-265",class:"mermaid",graph:"flowchart%20TD%0A%20%20R%5B%22A%20workflow%20is%20a%20pure%20reducer%2C%20so%20it%20never%20suspends%22%5D%0A%20%20R%20--%3E%20E%5B%22Nothing%20is%20held%20between%20events%20%E2%86%92%20efficient%22%5D%0A%20%20E%20--%3E%20T%5B%22No%20stack%20capture%2C%20sandbox%20or%20replay%20driver%20%E2%86%92%20tiny%22%5D%0A%20%20T%20--%3E%20G%5B%22A%20tiny%20kernel%20with%20no%20I%2FO%20runs%20anywhere%20wasm%20runs%20%E2%86%92%20built%20for%20the%20edge%22%5D%0A%20%20R%20--%3E%20P%5B%22State%20is%20rewritten%20with%20every%20event%2C%20so%20a%20resume%20is%20one%20read%20%E2%86%92%20performant%22%5D%0A%20%20R%20--%3E%20L%5B%22A%20pure%20function%20can%20be%20hashed%20and%20re-checked%2C%20one%20transaction%20per%20step%20%E2%86%92%20reliable%22%5D%0A"})]),fallback:a(()=>[...e[0]||(e[0]=[h(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[2]||(e[2]=o('<p>Three phrases carry the idea:</p><ul><li><strong>State is the snapshot.</strong> There is exactly one checkpoint per run, rewritten with every event. Recovery loads it, checks its hash and carries on.</li><li><strong>The journal is the queue.</strong> A task is a row committed in the same transaction as the event that asked for it.</li><li><strong>Nothing suspends.</strong> No function is ever paused mid-line, so there is no stack to save and no code to re-run.</li></ul><h2 id="how-this-differs-from-other-designs" tabindex="-1">How this differs from other designs <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-this-differs-from-other-designs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How this differs from other designs&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every durable engine must answer one question: when the process dies halfway, how does the work continue? There are four answers.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>How progress survives a crash</th><th>What it asks of you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Replayed code</strong></td><td>The workflow function runs again from the top. Recorded step results are replayed, so it fast-forwards to where it stopped.</td><td>Deterministic workflow code. Replay work grows with history. Mistakes are caught after the fact.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>State-machine definition</strong></td><td>The workflow is data, not code. The service stores the current state.</td><td>Logic in JSON or YAML. Branches and loops are verbose.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Snapshotted runtime</strong></td><td>The whole runtime instance is checkpointed and resumed.</td><td>A server that owns the runtime, and large snapshots.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Event-sourced reducer</strong> (Vlo)</td><td>Each event is folded into a state snapshot inside one transaction. There is nothing to replay.</td><td>Workflows written as pure reducers, or with the <code>sequence</code> helper. No <code>await</code> inside a workflow.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The first three keep the same assumption: a workflow is a running program that must be paused and brought back. They differ in how they bring it back. Vlo drops the assumption. A workflow is never running, so it never needs to be paused, saved or resumed. It is only ever asked one question: given this state and this event, what next?</p><h2 id="the-trade" tabindex="-1">The trade <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-trade" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The trade&quot;">​</a></h2><p>You give up writing a workflow as straight <code>await a(); await b();</code> code. In exchange, recovery never depends on re-running that code.</p><p>The <code>sequence</code> helper covers linear pipelines, so most workflows read as a list of steps:</p><div class="language-ts vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">ts</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">sequence</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">([</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">step</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(plan), </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">fanOut</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(work, selectItems, { concurrency: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">2</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> }), </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">waitFor</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(approve), </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">step</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(finish)]);</span></span></code></pre></div><p>An agent loop is one call to <code>agentLoop</code>. Reducers are plain functions, so you unit test them with no engine running. See <a href="./workflows">workflows.md</a> and <a href="./testing">testing.md</a>.</p><h2 id="what-vlo-does-not-have-yet" tabindex="-1">What Vlo does not have yet <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-vlo-does-not-have-yet" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What Vlo does not have yet&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Check this list before choosing it.</p><ul><li><strong>No console.</strong> There is no web UI with a run tree. Vlo has the journal, a live event stream, the HTTP API and the CLI. See <a href="./observability">observability.md</a>.</li><li><strong>Workflows are TypeScript only.</strong> Workers can be written in any language; the reducer runs in the Node host.</li><li><strong>No managed cloud.</strong> You run the host. <code>vlo deploy</code> renders the Cloud Run or Vercel files, but nobody operates it for you.</li><li><strong>No edge host.</strong> The Cloudflare host and Durable Object journal are planned, not built.</li><li><strong>Nothing enforces reducer purity.</strong> A TypeScript reducer is a plain function, so it could read the clock or call the network, and no lint catches that today. A mistake surfaces only when a run is rebuilt from its journal.</li><li><strong>Benchmarks are local only.</strong> One laptop, with the database on the same machine. There is no production or large-scale figure yet.</li><li><strong>Known weak spots.</strong> A step makes about 15 database round trips, so the host must sit beside its journal. One run that fans out very wide slows down sharply; wide work belongs in child runs. See <a href="./limits#known-weak-spots">limits.md</a>.</li><li><strong>It is young.</strong> Older engines have years of production history that Vlo does not.</li></ul><h2 id="when-to-pick-it" tabindex="-1">When to pick it <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-to-pick-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When to pick it&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Pick Vlo when:</p><ul><li>you want durable workflows and agents without operating a server;</li><li>your Postgres is already the system of record you trust;</li><li>workers live in more than one language or more than one place;</li><li>idle cost matters, because your runs spend most of their life waiting;</li><li>you want agents with budgets that park instead of fail.</li></ul><p>Pick a different engine when your team writes long linear workflows and wants them to read as plain <code>await</code> code, or when you need a console and a support contract on day one.</p><p>Next: <a href="./getting-started">getting-started.md</a>, or the common objections in <a href="./faq">faq.md</a>.</p>',19))])}const v=n(c,[["render",p]]);export{b as __pageData,v as default};
